{"id": "6e7507c3-5e3b-4d37-88ed-2493c691fada", "question": "does this flooring feel warmer than tiles?", "status": "ok", "num_iterations": 2, "total_delta_f": 0.9999999900000002, "total_step_score": 0.4999999950000001, "trajectory_quality_score": 0.24999999750000004, "steps": [{"iteration_index": 1, "retrieval_action": "text_retrieval_result", "retrieval_type": "Text Retrieval", "mode": "text_retrieval", "previous_query_for_similarity": null, "query": "comparison of warmth between wooden floors and tiles.", "query_similarity_enabled": true, "query_similarity_with_previous": null, "similarity_threshold": 0.85, "fact_similarity_threshold": 0.9, "retrieval_index": 3, "reaction_index": 4, "reaction_action": "thought", "reaction_text": "The retrieved documents provide information about the thermal properties of different flooring materials. Parquet floors are mentioned as being warmer underfoot, and underfloor heating is described as a method that can make flooring surfaces feel warmer. However, the documents do not directly compare the warmth of wooden floors to tiles. Therefore, we need to consider the general properties of these materials.\n</Thought>", "facts_t": ["Unpolished flooring surface materials and treatments have high emissivity (0.85 to 0.95).", "High emissivity flooring surfaces make good heat radiators.", "Underfloor heating and cooling (reversible floors) require flooring surfaces with high absorbance and emissivity and low reflectivity."], "filtered_facts_t": ["Underfloor heating and cooling (reversible floors) require flooring surfaces with high absorbance and emissivity and low reflectivity."], "accepted_facts_t": ["Underfloor heating and cooling (reversible floors) require flooring surfaces with high absorbance and emissivity and low reflectivity."], "delta_f": 0.9999999900000002, "novel_facts": ["Underfloor heating and cooling (reversible floors) require flooring surfaces with high absorbance and emissivity and low reflectivity."], "fact_novelty_mat